# Target C: the actual exterior cell admits a partial unit-wall shield

**Date:** 2026-10-06. **Authority:** `ae6697b649d5516cfe36cc855a89948940f9ce14`,
state `2026-10-06.audit.63`. **Worker:** 2, macroscopic exterior caps.
**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit; default
repository lifecycle unreviewed. C-II and Level I remain open.

This is an exterior-specific extension of the now canonical
`C-ONE-VERTEX-CONVEX-WALL-SHIELD`. Its originating research report is
Worker 1's `target_c_one_vertex_convex_wall_shield_2026-10-06.md` at
`258bd91a`; the exact promoted statement is Section 6 of audit.63,
`target_c_owner_order_and_terminal_capacity_promotion_audit_2026-10-06.md`.
The scope extension below is **not** part of that promotion.

## 1. Why this region is different from the old cap

The closing chord of an occupied actual cap is nonunit. It supplies no
unit-wall shield. Instead augment the actual core with the **full source
hull boundary**, and use the resulting outer-face cell containing the cap.
Its inner boundary consists of actual unit contacts; its other boundary
lies on the old source hull, through which a segment joining two source
points cannot exit early. The cell includes its inside-F outer pockets.

```text
actual cap -> convex cell of G union partial(K)
  -> actual unit inner walls + source-hull outer boundary
  -> one-wall-vertex strict shield, with all outside isolates checked
  -> exact local packet population |S|+1 pays one deleted core vertex
  -> contact/diameter contradiction if such a packet exists.
```

The last packet is not supplied by area or by the shield. This report
proves the actual container and full-source replacement criterion, then
records the exact capacity needed. It does not reprove Worker 1's internal
wall classification or claim a second internal-wall closure.

## 2. Actual convex cells after source-hull augmentation

Let G be the canonical connected positive-contact core, B all actual
diameter endpoints, F=conv(G)=conv(B), and K=conv(A). Canonical Section
67 supplies:

- every component of G-x contains a loaded vertex other than x;
- an unloaded core vertex has positive contact equilibrium and degree >=3;
- all core points on partial(F) are loaded;
- all actual diameter endpoints are extreme source-hull points.

Form the plane graph Gamma by adding the polygonal boundary of K to G.
Include every source point on partial(K) as a boundary-cycle vertex, even
if it is collinear on a hull side. Count coincident core/boundary edges
only once. Added boundary segments cannot cross the embedded G.

**Lemma M.1.** Gamma is 2-connected, and every bounded face is a convex
polygonal cell.

**Proof.** Remove a core vertex x. Every component of G-x has a loaded
representative other than x. The boundary cycle, or the boundary path if
x is on it, connects all those representatives and all remaining hull
vertices. If x is a hull isolate, G remains connected and the remaining
boundary path connects to G at loaded vertices. Thus Gamma-x is connected
in either case. Every bounded-face boundary is consequently a simple cycle.

At a hull vertex, every interior face angle is at most pi by convexity of
K. At any nonhull core vertex, a reflex face sector would put every
positive contact vector in its complementary open cone of width <pi.
Their positive equilibrium sum could not vanish; the vertex is unloaded
because every loaded vertex is on the source hull. Hence all face angles
are at most pi. A simple polygon with this property is convex. QED.

The new cells are precisely subdivisions of the old outer contact face
by the source-hull cycle. Original bounded contact faces are unchanged.
Each cell Q meeting the interior of an actual exterior cap C contains
the entire interior of C: there is no core vertex or edge outside F,
and the cap interior is connected and inside K. Hence a macroscopic C
provides an actual Q with

\[
 area(Q)=\Omega(D^2),\qquad |S_Q|=\Omega(D^2),            \tag{1}
\]

where S_Q is defined below and includes the cap's outer-hull isolates.

Call a boundary side of Q a **wall side** if it is an actual unit edge
of G, including a coincident source-hull edge. Every other side of Q
lies on partial(K). Let W_Q be the vertices incident with wall sides.
Every core boundary vertex of Q belongs to W_Q: if its two face edges
were both pure hull edges, any actual core edge at that vertex in K's
interior sector would intervene in that face traversal. All other source
points in Q are contact isolates. Put

\[
 S_Q=(A\cap Q)\setminus W_Q.                             \tag{2}
\]

This includes isolates on the outer boundary, not just int(Q). They must
be counted/deleted in a whole-cell replacement. There is no source point
in the interior of a wall segment, by unit separation from its endpoints.

The wall-side occurrences of Q belong to the old outer contact walk,
whose length is b<=2h<=2pi D. Its hull-side arcs total at most per(K).
Consequently per(Q)<=b+per(K)<=3pi D. We do not infer independent packet
capacities from the old cap/pocket decomposition: every contribution
assigned to an actual cell must be counted once, and packets in different
cells need their full cross-distance and shared-guard checks.

## 3. One unguarded actual wall vertex with an open hull boundary

Choose a vertex v of Q whose two incident Q sides are actual unit wall
sides, with neighbors a,b. Define

\[
 \mathcal W_{Q,v}=\{x\in int(Q):|x-w|>1
                 \text{ for all }w\in W_Q\setminus\{v\}\}. \tag{3}
\]

No distance guard is imposed at an isolate vertex on the source-hull
portion: those vertices belong to S_Q and are deleted.

**Theorem M.2 (exterior partial-wall shield).** Every x in (3) is
strictly farther than one from every original point of
A\(S_Q union {v}). This checks the entire retained source, including
all outside isolates.

**Proof.** Retained wall vertices are checked by (3). Every other
retained point q is outside Q and lies in K. A segment from x in int(Q)
to q remains in int(K) before q: convexity of K and x in int(K) prove
this even when q is on partial(K). Thus its first exit from Q before
q cannot use a pure source-hull side.

If |x-v|>1, x is strict-clear of all wall vertices. The first exit is
a wall side or retained wall vertex. At a unit side cd and exit point
z=(1-s)c+sd, the weighted-square identity gives

\[
 |x-z|^2>1-s(1-s)\ge3/4,\qquad
 |q-z|^2\ge1-s(1-s)\ge3/4.
\]

Since z lies between x and q, |x-q|>sqrt(3)>1. A retained-vertex exit
has |x-q|>2 by source separation.

For |x-v|<=1, use the exact local cone argument of canonical audit.63.
Translate v to zero, put r=|x|<=1, and let K_v be its convex cone with
unit boundary rays a,b. Strict clearance gives x dot a,x dot b<r^2/2.
For a q outside int(K_v), source separation |q-v|>=1 and the extremal
boundary-ray dot product imply |x-q|>1. For a q in int(K_v) but outside
Q, [x,q] stays in that cone's interior and cannot exit through the two
incident wall sides or through v. It also cannot exit early through the
source hull. Its first exit is therefore a retained unit side or retained
wall vertex; the preceding calculation applies. QED.

The reuse is precisely the promoted cone/retained-unit-exit implication.
The new reason a closed unit polygon is unnecessary is that every old
outside point is inside K, while the nonunit boundary lies on partial(K).
This is not a shield for arbitrary opened walls, two deleted vertices,
or a container with an unguarded nonunit side in the interior of K.

**Nonstrict version.** Replace the >1 wall inequalities in (3) by >=1.
Then the site is unit-clear of the entire retained source and is still
**strictly** clear of every retained nonwall point outside Q. All possible
new-old unit contacts are confined to the explicitly guarded wall vertices.

To check the only changed argument, the unit-side exit now gives a
distance >=sqrt(3)>1 and a retained-vertex exit a distance >=2>1. In
the vertex-cone case one has mu<=r^2/2 rather than a strict inequality.
For an outside-cone point q=t u, equality |x-q|=1 would require t=1,
mu=r^2/2, and u to be an extremal outside direction. Since x is strictly
inside the convex cone, its extremal outside directions are precisely
the boundary unit rays a,b. Hence equality would make q a wall neighbor,
already covered by its guard. Any distinct nonwall q is still strictly
clear. This finite extension permits exact-contact packets; it does not
equate nonstrict packet capacity with strict spare capacity.

## 4. Exact full-source cardinality criterion

**Theorem M.3.** If a finite unit-separated packet
Y subset mathcal W_{Q,v} has |Y|>=|S_Q|+1, a canonical triangle-free
global minimizer with this cell cannot exist.

**Proof.** Delete S_Q union {v}, select exactly |S_Q|+1 sites, and
dilate this finite packet by a sufficiently small factor >1. Its
interiority and strict wall guards persist; all internal unit distances
open. M.2 then checks every retained outside lower distance strictly.
All new points lie in int(K), so every pair involving them is strictly
shorter than D. Cardinality is unchanged. The contact graph is exactly
the induced old graph on the retained labels and remains triangle-free;
at least the two old wall contacts at v disappear. Diameter <D contradicts
global optimality, and diameter =D contradicts contact minimality. QED.

Thus each such actual cell satisfies the exact survivor inequality

\[
 \operatorname{Pack}_1(\mathcal W_{Q,v})\le|S_Q|,           \tag{4}
\]

where Pack_1 denotes the largest finite unit-separated packet cardinality.
This is a source-specific non-strict packet bound in an open guarded
region; it is not an Oler or area assertion. The exact closing threshold
is one integer above its **full** assigned isolate population, including
all outer-hull boundary points. An independently deleted actual-diameter
cover can be paid by the correspondingly larger packet, as in audit.63.

An occupied macroscopic cap does provide a qualifying v. If Q had no
nonhull core edge, its simple boundary would be the whole hull cycle,
so Q=K and all of G would lie on partial(K). Its positive contact graph
would be a subgraph of the loaded cycle, by canonical loaded adjacency.
The leaf/cycle equilibrium argument in earlier E.4 excludes this in the
presence of an occupied cap. Therefore Q has a nonhull core edge. Such
an edge has an unloaded endpoint: a loaded-loaded unit edge is a loaded
hull side, and a source point beyond that side is excluded by audit.62's
occupied-cap threshold. It is consequently also a source-hull side.
At the unloaded nonhull endpoint both Q sides are core unit edges. This
gives the claimed v. This uses E.4 only for its explicitly rederived
leaf/cycle implication; no new canonical promotion is claimed.

### Exact-contact ledger alternative

Let \(\mathcal N_{Q,v}\) be the nonstrict guarded region above. Delete
R=S_Q union {v} union T for any additional old labels T, and keep guards
at every original wall vertex other than v, even if T deletes that vertex.
Suppose Y subset mathcal N_{Q,v} is unit-separated and |Y|=|R|. The
nonstrict shield checks every retained outside source point. All pairs
involving Y are strictly below D. Let t count old contacts incident to R
and j count new contacts involving Y; these new contacts are only packet
contacts and retained wall anchors, so the ledger is local and exact.

If R covers all old actual diameters, this is a strict-sub-D global
competitor with no triangle-free or contact-budget restriction. Otherwise,
if the complete new contact graph is triangle-free and either j<t, or
j=t with an old actual diameter removed, the exact canonical contact/then
diameter-pair minimality excludes the endpoint. No opening or dilation is
required for this version. Every triangle through a retained wall anchor
must be checked explicitly; an internally triangle-free packet alone
does not check the full graph.

This alternative retains exact unit contacts when they pay the ledger.
It cannot be replaced by strict-safe area counting. Nor does it assert
that the packet exists, or that deleting several wall guards without
continuing to impose them is safe.

## 5. Composition limits and regressions

The enlargement includes the old inside-F pockets, so a packet in (3)
can use interior sites forbidden to a cap-only exchange. The old closing
chord is not imposed as an artificial packet boundary. Conversely,
sites remaining entirely outside int(F) still face the finite E.6
release barrier; merely enlarging the container supplies no capacity.

The partial-wall shield does not import the <=five-side emptiness or
physical-hexagon consequences: Q's source-hull sides are generally
nonunit, and those closed-unit-wall angle arguments do not apply.
Removing two wall vertices remains unprotected. The canonical square
and retained-isolate two-deletion regression is not bypassed.

Nor may a nonunit interior side be declared an unguarded hull boundary.
For an exact regression take the source

\[
 (0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(0,1),(2,1),\quad q=(1,17/10).
\]

All source pairs are unit-separated; q is a contact isolate and the
only contacts are the four bottom/vertical unit wall segments of the
rectangle [0,2] x [0,1]. Delete its bottom middle vertex v=(1,0).
The site x=(1,4/5) is strict-clear of the four remaining wall vertices
(squared distances 41/25 and 26/25), yet |x-q|^2=81/100<1. The
rectangle's nonunit top side is in the **interior** of the actual source
hull because q lies above it. This violates M.2's hull-boundary hypothesis.
For the actual cell of G union partial(K), q belongs to S_Q and its
cardinality must be paid; it cannot be silently retained or discarded.
The rational instance is preserved and independently checked in the
companion separator/refill certificate.

The strained near-capacity cloud and adaptive saturation remain binding:
neither (1) nor the missing guard at v proves |S_Q|+1 sites. The core
rhombus completion regression still blocks a generic interior-site
inference. The cap/core separator motion of the companion report is
not assumed compatible with keeping Q's wall fixed; a joint construction
would have to prove its complete new lower and upper constraints.

**Exact remaining packet obligation:** for at least one actual
cap-containing cell Q and qualifying actual wall vertex v, force
Pack_1(mathcal W_{Q,v})>=|S_Q|+1, or pay a simultaneous moved-core packet
by its full source cardinality, or construct a nonstrict guarded packet
with the complete ledger above. The first condition would close this
source by M.3; none of these conditions is established here. The broader paired-cap
nonlocal refill remains open. No universal strict spare site or cap
elimination is claimed.

**Validation:** proofs are finite and analytic, using canonical G-x
harmonic topology, loaded adjacency, and the audit.63 cone implication.
No new numerical script is required. All claims of this scope extension
remain unreviewed candidates, with exact provenance preserved above.
